# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keeping the live routing engine in step with the routing_rules table.
#
# custom_routing is GENERATED from the table (45_routing_rules.bcl). The table is
# the truth; the compiled definition is what routing actually reads. Two things
# can leave them apart:
#
#   a fresh database   the eight rules seeded in 01_database.bcl are rows, not
#                      compiled decisions, so until the first publish they are
#                      inert and every message is routed by the platform tiers
#                      alone
#   a lost override    the engine holds its rules in memory; a replica that came
#                      up without the stored source, or a restored file, is
#                      running rules the table no longer says
#
# Both are the failure an operator sees as "I switched that rule off and it is
# still routing that way", so the table is re-rendered on a timer rather than
# only when somebody edits a rule. The publish is fingerprinted
# (routing_published), so a tick that finds nothing changed costs one query and
# one UPDATE and recompiles nothing.
#
# 2s is the window in which a replica can be routing from a stale definition, and
# it is the only thing 2s buys: a tick with nothing to do is two small queries.
# An operator editing a rule does not wait for it at all — the console's save,
# enable and delete all publish in the request, so a switch takes effect before
# the response comes back.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

schedule "routing_publish" { every 2s queue "bus" intent "routing_rules.publish" }
